// The include comes FIRST here, unlike the usual advice, and that is deliberate.
//
// Options are merged in rules-file order and the last one to touch a key wins.
// With our rule after the include, caps:shift_modifier beats a stale stock caps
// option left over in gsettings; with it before, caps:none silently won and the
// option appeared to do nothing.  Verified both ways - see ../../../ReadMe.md.
//
// Nothing else needs a rule: the keymap is reached by the stock
//     *  *  =  pc+%l%(v)
// rule, which resolves de+prog to pc+de(prog) and lands in ../symbols/de.
! include %S/evdev

// The 'caps:' prefix is a UI grouping convention, not a file name - upstream
// mixes them freely too (caps:none is +capslock(none), caps:internal is a types
// rule +caps(internal)).  We still use capslock, the file the other 13 caps:
// options live in, so this line has the same shape as the stock ones and the
// grouping stays honest.
//
// The file was called 'custom' before.  That name is a trap: xkeyboard-config
// registers a layout named 'custom' in its own rules/evdev.xml, so GNOME offers
// "A user-defined custom Layout" in Settings whether or not you have such a
// file - and if you do, picking it compiles pc+custom, i.e. the first section
// here, into a keymap with modifiers but no letters.  The registry entry cannot
// be removed or renamed from ~/.config/xkb (libxkbregistry merges additively:
// new names are added, existing ones never overridden), so the fix is to not
// answer to that name.  'capslock' is a symbols file only, never a layout.
! option = symbols
caps:shift_modifier = +capslock(shift_modifier)

// us(intl) always picks up the AE11 override in ../symbols/us(ndash).
//
// After the include for the same reason the option is: by the time we get
// here the stock rule has already set symbols to  pc+us(intl), and a rule
// value starting with '+' appends to the current value instead of replacing
// it (+bar onto foo gives foo+bar).  Appending is also what makes the
// override win - a later include overrides an earlier one for the keys it
// defines - so this must come after, not before.
//
// One rule set per layout position, the same shape the stock symbols rules
// have: the unindexed set matches only when a single layout is configured,
// layout[N] matches position N when several are, and ':N' places the section
// in that group.  The sets do not overlap, so the section is appended once.
// libxkbcommon 1.8 would collapse all five into
//     ! layout[any]  variant[any]  =  symbols
//       us           intl          =  +us(ndash):%i
// but layout[any] and %i do not exist in 1.6.
! layout        variant         = symbols
  us            intl            = +us(ndash)

! layout[1]     variant[1]      = symbols
  us            intl            = +us(ndash):1

! layout[2]     variant[2]      = symbols
  us            intl            = +us(ndash):2

! layout[3]     variant[3]      = symbols
  us            intl            = +us(ndash):3

! layout[4]     variant[4]      = symbols
  us            intl            = +us(ndash):4
